;; slots-rules-testing.repl — the six MACHINES' RULES, pinned against independent literals, for
;; n_48867b242317a0216a67f8c7ca26696b5878e0e3.slots.
;;
;; What a player is promised is each machine's strip, its paytable, the return they add up to,
;; the most one spin can pay, and the recipe that turns a beacon into reel stops. Every one is
;; pinned here against a LITERAL produced outside this module, never against the constant under
;; test. Pure functions only: no round is opened, no coin moves.
;;
;;   M1  the machines: MACHINE-COUNT, SYMBOL-CODES, each MACHINES entry field by field, each
;;       strip's symbol counts recounted by this file's own fold over the literal strip, where
;;       code 5 may appear, and the neighbour property per strip
;;   M2  the exact return of every machine: RETURNS, recomputed here for the line and wild
;;       machines from the literal counts and a literal 216-entry pay list, the reduced fractions
;;       by cross-multiplication, EDGES, MAX-MULTS, and the smallest win of each machine
;;   M3  the evaluators: spin-units on 56 keys per machine against the oracle, every paytable
;;       category through line-pay / wild-pay, free-look, tumble-units with traced cascades, and
;;       the beacon-to-reels recipe (board-seed, spin-key, key-stops, play-board) per machine
;;   M4  the cap never binds: line/wild maximum over the 216 triples, the free-spins
;;       no-line-on-trigger property over all 64 stops, tumble's enumerated maximum; each with a
;;       control proving the check can fail
;;   M5  a board of several machines: play-entries on four multi-entry boards (one on
;;       all six machines) with spins numbered across entries, pinned stop for stop; a one-entry
;;       board equals play-board; entries-reserve and entries-fee as per-entry sums (M5's own
;;       provenance note says how its literals were produced)
;;
;; HOW THE LITERALS WERE PRODUCED. Python 3 only, never this module. A generator (`gen.py`)
;; writes this file from `rules_oracle.py`, `boards.py` and `hunt.py`, which import an
;; independent Python model of the machines written from their specification. Those scripts
;; are not published; the file names below say which part of that model each literal came from:
;;   - strips: `a_final.py` (classic = its `live` strip; easy = layout({4:2,3:4,2:10,1:12,0:36});
;;     big = layout({4:4,3:4,2:8,1:4,0:44})), `e_wild.mk(2)` (wild), `d_free.machine(P, 4)`
;;     (free-spins), `f_cascade.build({4:4,3:8,2:14,1:22,0:16})` (tumble);
;;   - pays: the P tuples of `a_final.py`, `e_wild40.py` (printed "pays (20, 8, 6, 15, 5, 2, 2, 40)"),
;;     the `d_free.py 40,20,10,10,5,3,2 4 8 2 24 100` argument, `f_cascade.py 5 x "{4:4,3:8,2:14,1:22,0:16}"`
;;     (printed "line pays C/L/B/7 = (7, 18, 35, 80)"); padded with 0 to eight slots (slot 7 is
;;     the wild's three-wild pay and is unused by the other kinds);
;;   - pay lists: `common.pay3` (a scatter or wild code 5 read as a blank on a line machine, where
;;     its count is 0) and `e_wild.mkpay(P, 'double')`, over the 216 triples a outermost;
;;   - returns: the weighted sums over those lists (asserted equal to a brute force over all
;;     262,144 stop triples); free-spins = the RTP fraction printed by the `d_free.py` command
;;     above, copied programmatically; tumble = "RTP=19651/20480" and "max=563/5" printed by the
;;     `f_cascade.py` command above, re-asserted by rules_oracle.py's own enumeration;
;;   - per-key units: common.pay3 / e_wild.mkpay / f_cascade.simulate / d_free's bonus recursion
;;     walked for one key; all 424 keys x 6 machines of `diff/py.json` reproduced exactly;
;;   - hashes and stops: `reproduce.py` (BLAKE2b-256, base64url unpadded, big-endian integer,
;;     stops = [h mod 64, (h div 64) mod 64, (h div 4096) mod 64]).

(load "slots-test-init.repl")

